he beginning to judge which region  of the sapphire would be perfect to  to follow uh yeah to select um a cell of  interest  on the right side you can see um  a six millimeter sapphire that  where we created an overview image in  two channels  in the green fluorescence channel but  also in  transmitted light and we did this  overview already with our 40x 1.1  and a sapphire corrected objective so  with the final magnification and  resolution  and it took us 486 um  tires to create this overview image and  imaging this in two channels took us  less than three minutes  because of the speed of the camera  as i said we are using here a specific  sapphire corrected objective  due to a very important reason  so the sapphire has different  imaging properties in comparison to  glass  so if we would use a standard glass  corrected objective  the the wavelength for example of the  green and of the red  yeah the green and the red wavelength  they  would not focus in the same  in the same focus position in in c  because of the  um of the grammatical aberration of the  sapphire  this would mean that the um the picture  in the end  would appear uh blurry and we wouldn't  be able to target very precise  with the sapphire corrected objective  the this chromatic aberration of the  sapphire is corrected so that the  different wavelengths  focus again at the same c  height inside the sample  the last thing i would like to highlight  is the transfer  mechanism as i said we use here a  three-step semi-automated  transfer uh to to  [Music]  decrease the time we need to  transfer our samples from the  light microscope into the high pressure  freezer  so we have first  a so-called slide to release mechanism  so we are sliding  the little mini incubator along  a ridge that is attached to the emi's  loading area with the sliding mechanism  the middle plate um detaches from  detaches from the mini incubator and  lays  directly into freezing roof then we have  the click to drop mechanism  where we have our lid application arm  with our closing carrier  that closes the whole sandwich and then  we use  the the already established close to  freeze mechanism of the emis  to initiate the freezing  this semi-automated transfer can be used  for left and  right-handed people and it can be also  upgraded on all existing  emis machines without  major modifications of the emis itself  i want to take one more minute to talk a  little bit about  time skills of biological processes  to put this five seconds transfer time  into a into relation  when we look at the different uh  biological processes  from very short to very long um timings  we see that the fastest fastest  events occur in a millisecond range  these events like a synaptic vesicle  fusion or  gpcr activation are not  catchable with these lifecycle  applications  anyway however here you could use the  established  emi slide and or electrical stimulation  to capture these types of events  then we have a transition phase between  one second  to five seconds which is more or less  critical for a vesicle transport  application and here it depends on your  biological question are you really  interested in the transport itself then  the five seconds a transfer time can be  a  limiting however if you're interested in  the the end result of a trend uh  transfer then we could potentially  capture this with the five  second transfer time all other um  [Music]  dynamic process like philippian dynamics  classroom  mediated endocytosis cell movements  mitosis and cytokinesis  they are more in the seconds to minutes  to our range  and are easily capturable with our  lifecyclem solution especially because  we can follow  these dynamic events from start until  the moment of interest  so i hope that i could show you that  with the new leica nano workflow we  could overcome  the obstacles that comes with livestream  experiments  like we can keep the samples in  physiological conditions at all  uh at all times thanks to the oxygenia  and our small sampling chambers we can  capture fast cellular  events with the thunder imager nano and  the  40x sapphire corrected objective  to identify and target our structure of  interest  we can obtain excellent fixation results  by high pressure freezing with our  established emis  high pressure freezer and we can  reliably retrieve the event of  interest in the electron microscope  thanks to our semi-automated  transfer mechanism and  with this i would like to thank you very  much um  for your attention and i'm looking  forward um  to our question and answer session and  with this i would like to hand over back  to jen  thank you dr kernig for your informative  presentation  we will now start with the live q a  portion of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  okay let's get started our first  question is  do the high pressure freezer and the  microscope need to be next to each other  at all times  yeah thank you very much um no they  don't need to be next to each other at  all times they can be  um in different labs or in different  facilities they don't need to be on the  same floor the emis is movable so and  can be for the experiments  can be transported next to the light  microscope  and then placed back to its original  point or parking position where you can  then do your  normal or standard high pressure  freezing experiments  all right thank you dr kernick our next  question is  would the workflow work also with other  imaging systems like confocal  yes so um we  used for our experiments we used the  thunder imager  uh because of its flexibility however if  your samples require confocal imaging  you also could perform the workflow on a  confocal like our new stellaris platform  great thank you again dr kernick our  next question is  are the sam sampling excuse me the  sampling  chambers one time use  um partially so you can  reuse parts like the middle plate of the  emis  and the covers who close the little mini  incubator  however the sapphire and also the  sampling base cup  they are a one-time use and they should  be replaced after each  experiment um  maybe one more thing i would like to  mention here is we offer then also  dedicated consumable packages  um that gives you or provides you  everything that is needed  for a certain amount of experimental  runs  thanks again dr kernick our next  question i'm going to try to pronounce  everything there are some abbreviations  and i think that's what this  uh question is wanting to clarify  on the 40x object so obj  mode objective mode correct  is mentioned what does the mot or the  moat mean  something motorized on the objective yes  so  exactly the objective has on top a  motorized scoring  the curing is needed to correct  some um unevenness of the sapphire when  when sapphires are produced  then it's not so easy to have them um as  plain as  our high quality cover glasses so  having a motorized correction ring will  help you  to even the slight differences in the  thickness of the  sapphire out  perfect thank you dr kernig our next  question is  is the idea to unload the sample from  the light microscope  load it into the high pressure freezer  and freeze it all within five seconds  yes exactly that that's the idea and it  actually works  so um the five seconds account from  taking the sample  at the microscope placing it onto the  high pressure freezer and freeze it  so this we call five second and transfer  time  all right thank you again dr kernig  this person asks could you please give  additional  details of how you identify and select  the cell of interest  after freeze substitution oh yeah i'm  sorry that i  missed this point so of course um at the  first step  we have our sapphire and we evaporate a  grid pattern this is um  um yeah we evaporate a grid pattern on  top of the  sapphire this grid pattern you can see  then in the transmitted light  of the light microscope and you see it  after  um free substitution and embedding  inside  the um on top of the  resin block so what we normally do in  the workflow is then and it's also  described in the  in the application um workflow  application node  that we then do um imaging an imaging  step  of the sapphire block  on the microscope and then we overlay  the block face with the light microscope  to see that all cells are still there  and then we go on to the sectioning you  don't need to do this step  but we only want to ensure that  everything is okay that our um  our cells are still there and then do  the  the subsequent sectioning and trimming  all right thank you again dr kernig the  next question  is would drug treatment experiments be  possible  yes they are possible  with some slight modifications but  in theory there that's not a big problem  for the  workflow to also do drug treatments  great and our next question is  is the light microscopy diffraction  limited  is there any possibility of sim or  single particle  tracking what is the detector for the  light microscopy  well that's a very tricky question for  me  as a non non-expert in light microscopy  however i try to answer it as as much as  i can so  the the sapphire differs in some imaging  properties  from the from  a standard glass so it's for example  double breaking which  makes dic not possible and also stat  imaging  is not possible if now structured  illumination  is uh possible with this um sapphire i  need to  check um with my r d team and  and yeah come back with this um question  at the moment uh  we use a a cmos camera on the microscope  for imaging but i think here um we are  totally flexible as we are totally  flexible from the imaging system  you could i think also use different  detection systems  like we have it on on confocal systems  for example  all right great thank you again dr  kernick the next question is  will the objective be available  separately  uh no not at the moment so this  sapphire corrected objective is  currently only available um  with this with the leica nano workflow  and the next question here is wouldn't  it be better to have a rigid connection  of the light microscope and the high  pressure freezer to reduce the transfer  time  um that was also for us very interesting  discussion internally and in the end we  decided  not to do it because of multiple reasons  so if we would have a rigidness a  connection between the light microscope  and the high pressure freezer  we may have would save two seconds or  potentially three seconds  and transfer time however as i showed  you before  in the slides the biological relevance  between two  or five seconds transfer time is is not  very high  on the other hand if we would have this  rigid connection  it would um take the  the advantages that we have with this  workflow a way for example  we wouldn't be as flexible with the led  lab setup so the microscope and the  high pressure freezer they would need to  be next to each other at all times  and also when it comes to imaging  modalities as i said  we we showed this here with the thunder  system but it also would work on a  confocal  this this wouldn't be so easily possible  anymore  and uh also immersion imaging  physiological control  um these are all parts that are then not  as easy anymore as uh yeah  as we have it now so in the end we  decided  not to do it and have the advantages on  the on the imaging  side and on the on the sample side  all right thank you again dr kernick the  next question is  what samples can be used within the  workflow besides cells  uh yeah in theory everything that fits  in the  um in the in the in the freezing  sandwich so we use here a  cover carrier with a hundred micron  steps  and we have six millimeter um space in  diameter  of the of the sapphire so everything  that fits into the six millimeter  and is not higher than hundred microns  um should work  with this workflow  thank you again dr kernick our next  question is  how easy is it to place an  ultrastructural  snapshot in the temporal contact if you  don't have zero seconds transfer time  yeah as i um showed this already before  in the  present in the presentation the idea of  the live cycle  is that you target a particular time or  stage  of a cellular process you are interested  in and  this time point is mostly a structural  change that you would like to  analyze then on the on the en side  so the most important question you need  to ask yourself is how  stable is this particular stage  that you are interesting is in and for  the majority of the applications  that we are aiming um with this workflow  or addressing with this workflow um  zero two three or five seconds is  not so um relevant however if you have  something  fast uh for example as i said this  tracking  um trafficking um  um yeah the trafficking um topics  these can a little bit more tricky and  there we have to look in  in detail um with the users what they  like to  achieve in the workflow  all right thank you again dr kernick our  last question is  do you have additional information or  videos  um yes we prepared uh  tons of material um that we will release  within the next  weeks so we have um different  application notes uh that gives you more  um  depth of knowledge on on the  physiological control of the samples why  is this important  and how the the sampling chamber is  designed  we have an application note on the  imaging part  that explains a little bit more deeper  how um  how the sapphire corrected objective  works um why we developed this and shows  you more examples  uh on different samples how the the  quality looks like  and we have a a very detailed workflow  protocol that  shows on an example um how we did the  experiment  how much cells we seeded how we coded  the samples  how we processed them how we cut them  and and all these  types furthermore there's a workflow  protocol that  gives you a step-by-step description  throughout  what you have to do on the technical  side  and for this workflow protocol we also  created a lot of videos that explain  more or less  every step that we described in the  manual also in the video  and this will be then also available via  the website  or later also on you on youtube and um  yeah  you can um find them then um  everything is then on the website and  you get also the notifications  in the newsletter  well thank you dr kernig do you have any  final comments for our audience today  yes i would like to thank everybody who  joined the webinar today  it was a pleasure for me um to  show you our new solution and answer all  your questions  and yeah if you have more questions  reach out to your regional  leica contact and we're looking forward  to hear from you  thank you again excuse me dr kernick for  your time today  and for your important research we would  also like to thank labyrinth and our  sponsor leica microsystems for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go  i'd like to thank the audience for  joining us today and for their  interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye


